C('Punishment vs. Discipline (Discipleship)','The word discipline comes from "disciple", a learner. Punishment makes a child pay; discipline helps a child learn.',
[['Punishment',0],['Discipline (discipleship)',150]],
[['Goal','Make the child suffer for what they did','Teach the child what to do next time'],['Focus','The past','The future'],['Child feels','Fear, shame, resentment','Safe, guided, responsible'],['Parent\'s state','Often angry or reactive','Calm and connected (or comes back to repair)'],['Typical tools','Shouting, threats, smacking, harsh isolation, taking everything away','Limits, natural and logical consequences, problem-solving, repair'],['Child learns','Avoid getting caught; big people hurt when angry','How to make it right; how to handle feelings'],['Picture of God','A harsh judge to be feared','A Father who trains for our good (Hebrews 12:10-11)']],
null,'Shame-Based vs. Grace-Based Correction;Gentle Parenting Styles Compared;Grace vs. Shame');
C('Gentle Parenting Styles Compared','Research on parenting styles (Baumrind and later studies) shows warm, firm parenting gives the best results.',
[['Authoritarian',0],['Permissive',36],['Gentle-authoritative',150],['Neglectful',220]],
[['Warmth','Low','High','High','Low'],['Limits','High, often harsh','Low or inconsistent','Clear, kind, consistent','Few or none'],['Sounds like','"Because I said so."','"Okay, whatever you want."','"I hear you. The answer is still no, and here\'s why."','"Do what you like."'],['Children often','Obey from fear; may rebel or hide','Struggle with limits and frustration','Feel secure, confident, self-controlled','Feel unseen; take risks'],['God\'s pattern','Law without grace','Grace without truth','Grace and truth together (John 1:14)','Absence']],
null,'Punishment vs. Discipline (Discipleship);Care vs. Control');
C('Downstairs Brain vs. Upstairs Brain','Dan Siegel and Tina Payne Bryson\'s picture: a child\'s brain is like a house still being built.',
[['Downstairs brain',0],['Upstairs brain',220]],
[['Where','Brainstem and emotional brain (amygdala)','Prefrontal cortex'],['Job','Survival, big feelings, fight-flight-freeze','Planning, empathy, self-control, problem-solving'],['Development','Fully working from birth','Under construction until the mid-twenties'],['In a meltdown','In charge','Offline (the "flipped lid")'],['What helps','Safety, calm presence, touch, few words','Questions, choices, reasoning, teaching'],['Parent tip','Connect first','Redirect once calm']],
null,'Flipping Your Lid: The Hand Model;Tantrums and Meltdowns');
C('Shame-Based vs. Grace-Based Correction','How we correct shapes what a child believes about themselves and about God.',
[['Shame-based',330],['Grace-based',150]],
[['Message','"You are bad."','"What you did was wrong, and you are loved."'],['Words','"What is wrong with you?" "You always…"','"That wasn\'t okay. Let\'s make it right."'],['Child hides or confesses?','Hides and lies','Confesses and repairs'],['Inner voice they develop','Harsh critic','Kind but honest coach'],['Picture of God','Disappointed and distant','Holy and near, quick to forgive (1 John 1:9)']],
null,'Grace vs. Shame;Guilt vs. Shame vs. Embarrassment;Lying');

X('decoder','Behind the Behaviour: Decoder','Ross Greene · Dan Siegel & Tina Payne Bryson · John Gottman',
'Choose your child\'s age and what they are doing. The decoder shows what is likely happening behind the behaviour, what your child needs, and what to try now.',
'Your seven-year-old screams "I hate you!" when you turn off the TV. Under the words: a flooded brain, loss of control and disappointment. They need calm limits and connection, then a repair conversation later.',
'Pick an age and a behaviour you see often;Read what is behind it before reacting;Try the "say this" sentence next time;Read the full page and the age guide',
'Gentle Parenting Styles Compared;Punishment vs. Discipline (Discipleship);Downstairs Brain vs. Upstairs Brain','connect ecoach kidrepair',
'A guide, not a diagnosis. If your child\'s behaviour worries you, talk to a doctor, school counsellor or child psychologist.');
